{
  "hintForAIAgent": "This is an Excalidraw diagram (JSON). This file is large and verbose; the `excalisketch` CLI lets you work with it compactly without loading the whole thing or hand-managing ids/bindings. START HERE: run `npx excalisketch --help` once — it lists every tool and the record format in one place. Workflow: `tool view <file>` prints a compact one-line-per-element view (ids, positions, text, edges) — read this instead of the raw JSON; `tool get <file> <id...>` shows full detail (incl. styles) for specific elements; `tool apply <file> <patch>` merges edits back by id — pass a .jsonl file, inline JSON record(s) as arguments, or stdin. For a connector, give an arrow only `from`/`to` and OMIT x/y/points (the tool positions it); add `points` (3+) only for custom routing. It keeps `boundElements` in sync for you. Add new elements, or remove one with {\"id\":\"X\",\"delete\":true}; `tool arrange <file>` auto-lays-out the graph; `tool render <file> --out out.png` renders an image so you can SEE the result (catch overlapping shapes or arrows that miss their targets — the JSON won't show this); `tool validate <file>` checks consistency (also run this if you hand-edit the JSON). Docs: https://github.com/Kirill89/excalisketch#tools-for-ai-agents. (The `source` and `hintForAIAgent` fields are added by excalisketch and are safe to ignore.)",
